card T2, they can still have the T4-T5-T6 chow. Since the player across has T1-T2-T3, maybe T2 is less likely to be needed. But the unshown T2 count is 2, so there are two left. The user has one, so discarding it might be safe.\n\nAnother consideration is the game history. The user previously played B7, F3, B1, T9. The next player has played B7, Chi B7, etc. The player across has played T2, T3, etc. The previous player has played B3, J2, etc. This suggests that some tiles are more commonly discarded. For example, B7 was played by the user and the next player. So maybe B7 is more common, but the user's hand doesn't have B7.\n\nThe user's valid actions include Play T6. T6 is part of the T4-T5-T6 chow. Discarding T6 would break that chow, which is a key part of the hand. So better to avoid that.\n\nSo the best options are to discard W6, W2, T2, F4, J2, etc. Among these, W6 and W2 are not part of chows. T2 is part of a potential chow but the user can still have T4-T5-T6. F4 is a wind tile but not as valuable as F2.\n\nIf the user discards W6, they can keep the T4-T5-T6 chow intact and the F2 pair. That seems good. Alternatively, discarding W2 would also be safe.\n\nBetween W6 and W2: which one is safer? The user has W2 in their hand. The player across has T1-T2-T3, which uses T2. The unshown T2 count is 2, so there are two left. The user has one, so if they discard it, another player might have one. But since the count is low, maybe it's safer to keep it. Alternatively, W6 has a higher count, so discarding it is safer.\n\nSo maybe Play W6 is a better choice. It doesn't affect the chows and reduces the number of tiles. The user can then focus on forming the T4-T5-T6 chow and other potential chows.\n\nAnother angle: the user's hand has F2 (two), which is a pair. If they discard W6, they can keep the pair and work on forming chows. The T4-T5-T6 is a good chow, and if they can form another chow with T2 and T3 (but T3 is in the player across's meld) or T5-T6-T7 (if they get 